('e03d67c9-e695-58f2-b268-8f14d609df17', 'aae53119-5afd-4055-b81b-60e6244772d7', '0d4f60c0-0c46-45a6-886d-ead834200dc8', 'a-level', '29.7', '9702 coursebook 29.7 · Decay graphs and equations', 40, 40, 'A count rate falls from 1600 to 100 counts per minute in 1.0 hour. Calculate the half-life, in s.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '900', 's', false, NULL, false, NULL, '1600 → 100 is a factor of 16, four half-lives: t½ = 3600 ÷ 4 = 900 s. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: factor of 16. C1: hour to seconds. A1: t½ = 900 s.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('125ba835-9914-51bb-9bbe-2a196b5a184a', 'aae53119-5afd-4055-b81b-60e6244772d7', '0d4f60c0-0c46-45a6-886d-ead834200dc8', 'a-level', '29.7', '9702 coursebook 29.7 · Decay graphs and equations', 41, 41, 'A source has a decay constant of 0.0462 min⁻¹. Its count rate (background removed) is 640 counts per minute now. What will it be after 45 minutes, in counts per minute?', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '80.04', 'counts per minute', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'C = 640 × e^(−0.0462 × 45) = 80.0 counts per minute. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: C = C₀e^(−λt). C1: λt in consistent units. A1: C = 80.0 counts per minute.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('7625acba-abc5-5342-9723-9e565b18255e', 'aae53119-5afd-4055-b81b-60e6244772d7', '0d4f60c0-0c46-45a6-886d-ead834200dc8', 'a-level', '29.7', '9702 coursebook 29.7 · Decay graphs and equations', 42, 42, 'The graph of ln N against t for a sample has a gradient of −1.2 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹ and an intercept of 30.0. Calculate the initial number of undecayed nuclei.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.069e13', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'N₀ = e^30.0 = 1.07 × 10¹³. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: N₀ = e^(intercept). C1: value. A1: N₀ = 1.07 × 10¹³.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('f31ae031-7be6-55be-99a4-28a4ec1bbc6c', 'aae53119-5afd-4055-b81b-60e6244772d7', '0d4f60c0-0c46-45a6-886d-ead834200dc8', 'a-level', '29.7', '9702 coursebook 29.7 · Decay graphs and equations', 43, 43, 'The activity of a source is measured every 20 s: t = 0, 20, 40, 60, 80 s gives A = 820, 560, 380, 260, 180 Bq. (a) Explain why ln A should be plotted against t. (b) Use the first and last readings to find λ. (c) Calculate the half-life. Give only the answer to (c).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '36.57', 's', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) ln A = ln A₀ − λt gives a straight line of gradient −λ. (b) λ = ln(820 ÷ 180) ÷ 80 = 0.01895 s⁻¹. (c) t½ = 0.693 ÷ 0.01895 = 36.6 s. Mark scheme (6 marks). B1: linear form. C1: ln values. M1: gradient. C1: λ. C1: t½. A1: t½ = 36.6 s.', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 316" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgf01d9700-t fgf01d9700-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgf01d9700-t">Activity readings</title><desc id="fgf01d9700-d">Table and graph of activity against time every 20 s: 820, 560, 380, 260 and 180 Bq, falling exponentially.</desc><g transform="translate(0,0)"><rect x="47.9" y="8" width="304.2" height="56" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="47.9" y="8" width="304.2" height="28" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="81.2" y="22" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">t / s</text><text x="138.2" y="22" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">0</text><line x1="114.5" y1="8" x2="114.5" y2="64" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejo